1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,218
Today, Nvidia is worth trillions of dollars.

2
00:00:03,438 --> 00:00:06,398
But in 1996, it almost died.

3
00:00:08,768 --> 00:00:14,177
It started in 1993, when three engineers sat down at a Denny's in San Jose.

4
00:00:14,397 --> 00:00:18,517
Video games will need 3D graphics. Let's build the chip!

5
00:00:18,987 --> 00:00:22,086
Their first chip, the NV1, was a disaster.

6
00:00:22,456 --> 00:00:25,462
It drew curves, but the industry chose triangles.

7
00:00:26,132 --> 00:00:30,043
Even worse, Sega had paid them to build the chip for its next console.

8
00:00:30,263 --> 00:00:32,608
So Jensen told Sega's boss the truth.

9
00:00:32,878 --> 00:00:36,218
Our design is wrong. You should find another partner.

10
00:00:36,538 --> 00:00:38,526
But if you don't pay us, we're finished.

11
00:00:39,596 --> 00:00:41,545
...Okay.

12
00:00:42,115 --> 00:00:46,004
Sega paid. Those five million dollars kept Nvidia alive.

13
00:00:46,374 --> 00:00:49,287
But Nvidia still had to lay off more than half its people.

14
00:00:49,657 --> 00:00:51,432
They had money for just one last chip.

15
00:00:51,902 --> 00:00:54,177
If it failed, Nvidia was dead.

16
00:00:54,947 --> 00:01:00,646
In 1997, the RIVA 128 sold a million units in four months.

17
00:01:01,116 --> 00:01:03,646
Nvidia survived. And that was only the beginning.

